. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. * FILTERS *  
.  
.         * none
. 
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES *  
. 
.         * see above, variables generated as part of data preparation
. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 *  
. 
.         * We run a logistic mixed effects model with random intercepts at the
>  individual (pid) and household (cid) levels.
.         * We predict if the last word on financial decisions in the household
>  (used to operationalize gender equality in the 
.         * household) is shared (1 = shared, 0 = unilateral) from whether the 
> person has daughters (1 = yes, 0 = everything else), 
.         * controlling for a year-fixed effect.
. 
.         *       xtmelogit household_GEM hasdaughters syear || cid: || pid:, i
> ntpoints(3)
. 
.         
.         * Because the model is not fully computationally reproducible, we cha
> nge the estimation approach. We instead use a logistic
.         * panel regression with random effects at the level of participants (
> pid). 
.         * We predict if the last word on financial decisions in the household
>  (used to operationalize gender equality in the 
.         * household) is shared (1 = shared, 0 = unilateral) from whether the 
> person has daughters (1 = yes, 0 = everything else), 
.         * controlling for a year-fixed effect.
.         
.         xtset pid

